      <section id="introduction" class="section">
        <p>The <strong>EU aerosol dispensers directive</strong>, formally Directive 75/324/EEC, is the harmonized European law governing the design, pressure testing, filling, and labeling of aerosol dispensers placed on the EU market. It works by setting mandatory technical construction standards and conformity marking requirements that every aerosol manufacturer or importer must satisfy before a product can be legally sold in EU member states. This matters for aerosol manufacturers because non-compliance blocks market access entirely and exposes companies to regulatory enforcement, making early compliance planning essential for any product destined for Europe.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section id="scope" class="section">
        <h2>Scope of the EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive</h2>
        <p>Directive 75/324/EEC applies to any non-reusable container made of metal, glass, or plastic that is filled with compressed, liquefied, or dissolved gas, and fitted with a release device that discharges its contents as a spray, foam, paste, or powder. This broad definition captures cosmetic aerosols, household cleaning sprays, industrial lubricants, paints, and technical products alike, spanning nearly every category of aerosol manufactured for the EU market.</p>
        <p>Certain product categories fall outside the directive's direct scope and are instead governed by separate, more specific EU frameworks. Medicinal aerosols, such as metered dose inhalers, are regulated under pharmaceutical legislation, while cosmetic-specific ingredient safety and claims are governed by the EU Cosmetics Regulation. Manufacturers producing multi-category products, such as a cosmetic aerosol, must therefore satisfy both the aerosol dispensers directive for container and pressure compliance and the relevant product-category regulation for formulation and labeling claims. For a broader view of how EU rules interact with cosmetic aerosol formulation, see our <a href="/cosmetics-and-personal-care-aerosols-guide/" target="_top">cosmetics and personal care aerosols guide</a>.</p>
        <p>Understanding this scope early in product development prevents costly redesign later. Manufacturers exporting into the EU from outside markets should map their product portfolio against the directive's definitions before finalizing container specifications, as detailed further in our <a href="/aerosol-manufacturing-101-guide/" target="_top">aerosol manufacturing 101 guide</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Diagram Image (PLACEHOLDER — see frontmatter for generation prompt) -->
