      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="pressure-testing" class="section">
        <h2>EU Aerosol Pressure Testing Requirements</h2>
        <p>EU aerosol pressure testing is central to demonstrating compliance with Directive 75/324/EEC, since container integrity under thermal stress is the primary safety concern for pressurized dispensers. The directive requires filled containers to be immersed in a hot water bath maintained at 50°C for a specified duration, verifying that the container does not leak, distort, or burst under the elevated internal pressure this temperature produces. This test simulates realistic worst-case storage and transport conditions, such as a vehicle interior or warehouse exposed to summer heat.</p>
        <p>Alongside the hot water bath test, manufacturers must verify that the internal pressure of the filled container at 50°C remains within defined limits relative to the container's rated burst pressure, ensuring an adequate safety margin. These limits vary based on container material and construction, with metal aerosol cans, glass containers, and plastic dispensers each subject to specific design and pressure thresholds under the directive's technical annexes.</p>
        <div class="table-container" style="overflow-x: auto; margin: 24px 0;">
          <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; text-align: left; font-size: 0.95rem;">
            <thead>
              <tr style="background: var(--primary); color: white;">
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Test Parameter</th>
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Requirement Under 75/324/EEC</th>
                <th style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Purpose</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr style="background: #f8fafc;">
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Hot Water Bath Test</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Immersion at 50°C for defined hold period</td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Verifies no leakage, distortion, or bursting under thermal stress</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Internal Pressure at 50°C</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Must remain within container-rated safety margin</td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Confirms adequate margin below burst pressure</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="background: #f8fafc;">
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Fill Ratio Verification</strong></td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Liquid phase must not fully occupy container at 50°C</td>
                <td style="padding: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--border);">Prevents hydraulic overpressure from thermal expansion</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Manufacturers seeking the underlying pressure test methodologies referenced across international aerosol standards can consult <a href="https://www.astm.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM International</a>, whose test methods for aerosol fill and pressure characteristics are frequently cross-referenced during EU compliance testing. For a detailed breakdown of how burst pressure testing protocols compare across jurisdictions, see our guide on <a href="/aerosol-burst-pressure-testing-iso-dot-protocols/" target="_top">aerosol burst pressure testing</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="labeling" class="section">
        <h2>Aerosol Labeling Under EU Regulation</h2>
        <p>Aerosol labeling EU regulation requirements under Directive 75/324/EEC work alongside the CLP Regulation (Classification, Labelling and Packaging), which implements the Global Harmonized System within the EU. Every compliant aerosol label must display the reversed epsilon conformity symbol, the net content by volume, and precautionary statements specific to pressurized containers, including warnings against exposure to heat above 50°C, puncturing, and incineration even when empty.</p>
        <p>Beyond the directive-specific pressure warnings, labels must carry GHS hazard pictograms and signal words corresponding to the product's classification under CLP, covering flammability, health hazards, and environmental hazards where applicable. Precautionary (P-statement) and hazard (H-statement) codes must be printed in the official language(s) of each EU member state where the product is sold, which often requires multi-language label variants for pan-European distribution. To understand the full pictogram and statement system referenced here, see our guide on <a href="/ghs-hazcom-labeling-aerosol-products/" target="_top">GHS and HazCom labeling for aerosol products</a>.</p>
      </section>

