      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section id="conformity-marking" class="section">
        <h2>CE Marking and the Reversed Epsilon Symbol</h2>
        <p>A common point of confusion for manufacturers new to the EU market is CE marking aerosol product expectations. Unlike most consumer product categories that carry the standard CE mark under general EU product safety law, aerosol dispensers compliant with Directive 75/324/EEC instead display the reversed epsilon symbol — a stylized "3" or reversed "E" mark — as their conformity indicator. This symbol certifies that the container's design, filling process, and pressure testing satisfy the directive's technical requirements.</p>
        <p>The reversed epsilon mark is a legal declaration by the manufacturer or importer that the aerosol dispenser has undergone the required design verification and testing procedures. It must appear clearly and permanently on the container, typically alongside the net content declaration, and cannot be substituted with a general CE mark, since the aerosol dispensers directive operates as a distinct, self-contained conformity system within EU product law.</p>
        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--primary); background: #f0f9ff; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--primary); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Key Insight</strong>
          The reversed epsilon symbol is specific to Directive 75/324/EEC and is not interchangeable with the general CE mark — aerosol manufacturers should not assume standard CE marking procedures apply to pressurized dispenser compliance.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section id="flammability" class="section">
        <h2>Flammability Classification & CLP Alignment</h2>
        <p>Flammability classification under the EU aerosol framework is determined through standardized ignition testing that measures flame projection distance, flashback distance, and the presence of flammable components above defined concentration thresholds. Based on these results, products are classified as non-flammable, flammable, or extremely flammable, a tiering system that determines which hazard pictograms and precautionary statements must appear on the finished label under the CLP Regulation.</p>
        <p>This classification also has downstream implications beyond labeling, directly influencing warehouse storage segregation and transport documentation. Products classified as extremely flammable under the EU framework typically align with the highest hazard tiers used in warehouse fire protection standards, meaning formulation choices made for EU flammability classification often echo requirements seen in storage codes elsewhere. For a comparison of flammability-driven storage classification, see our guide on <a href="/nfpa-30b-aerosol-warehouse-storage-classification/" target="_top">NFPA 30B warehouse storage classification</a>. Formulators evaluating solvent and propellant flash point data during EU classification testing can reference the <a href="https://echa.europa.eu/regulations/clp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ECHA CLP Regulation</a> portal directly.</p>
        <div class="box" style="border-left: 4px solid var(--amber); background: #fffbeb; padding: 20px; margin: 24px 0; border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;">
          <strong style="color: var(--amber); display: block; margin-bottom: 6px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif;">Rule of Thumb</strong>
          Run flammability classification testing early in formulation development, since a shift from "flammable" to "extremely flammable" changes label pictograms, transport documentation, and warehouse storage requirements simultaneously.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section id="compliance-documentation" class="section">
        <h2>Compliance Documentation & Market Surveillance</h2>
        <p>European aerosol directive compliance requires manufacturers or the entity placing the product on the EU market to maintain comprehensive technical documentation, including pressure test results, formulation flammability data, container specification sheets, and quality control records from the filling process. This documentation must be retained and made available to national market surveillance authorities upon request, forming the evidentiary basis for the reversed epsilon conformity declaration.</p>
        <p>Market surveillance authorities across EU member states can conduct spot checks on aerosol products already in circulation, and non-compliant products can be subject to recall or sales prohibition. Given the cross-border nature of EU distribution, a single compliance failure can trigger enforcement action across multiple member states simultaneously, making thorough documentation practices a critical part of any European market entry strategy. For guidance on the full documentation package expected during regulatory review, see our resource on <a href="/understanding-sds-tds-and-coa-for-aerosol-products/" target="_top">understanding SDS, TDS, and COA for aerosol products</a>. The full legal text of the directive itself is published on <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX%3A31975L0324" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">EUR-Lex</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- FAQ Section -->
      <section id="faq" class="box" style="background: #ffffff; border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 30px; margin: 40px 0;">
        <h2 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 24px; font-family: 'Outfit', sans-serif; color: var(--text-primary);">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px;">
          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What products does the EU Aerosol Dispensers Directive 75/324/EEC cover?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Directive 75/324/EEC covers all aerosol dispensers placed on the EU market, meaning any non-reusable container made of metal, glass, or plastic, filled with compressed, liquefied, or dissolved gas, fitted with a release device that discharges its contents as a spray, foam, paste, or powder. This includes cosmetic, household, industrial, and technical aerosol products, though certain categories such as medicinal aerosols are regulated under separate pharmaceutical directives.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What pressure testing is required under the EU aerosol dispensers directive?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The directive requires that aerosol dispensers withstand a hot water bath test at 50°C for a minimum period without leaking, distorting, or bursting, verifying that internal pressure at that elevated temperature stays within the container's design limits. Manufacturers must also verify that the filled container's internal pressure at 50°C does not exceed 12 bar for most metal containers, with specific limits varying by container material and construction.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Does an aerosol product need CE marking under 75/324/EEC?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Aerosol dispensers that comply with Directive 75/324/EEC carry the reversed epsilon symbol on the label rather than the CE mark used for most other product categories under EU law. This symbol certifies that the dispenser meets the directive's design, filling, and testing requirements, and it is a mandatory conformity marking for aerosol products legally placed on the EU market.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">What labeling information is mandatory on an EU-compliant aerosol can?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Mandatory labeling includes the reversed epsilon conformity symbol, flammability classification symbols where applicable, precautionary statements regarding pressurized containers (such as warnings against puncturing, heat exposure, and disposal), the net content by volume, and GHS hazard pictograms and signal words corresponding to the product's classification under the CLP Regulation.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); padding-bottom: 16px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">How does the EU aerosol directive classify flammability for labeling purposes?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">Flammability classification under the directive follows criteria based on flame projection distance during ignition testing, flashback distance, and the presence of flammable components exceeding defined thresholds. Products are classified as non-flammable, flammable, or extremely flammable, with each tier requiring corresponding GHS pictograms and precautionary statements under the CLP Regulation, which implements GHS within the EU.</p>
          </div>

          <div style="padding-bottom: 8px;">
            <h4 style="font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--text-primary);">Who is responsible for demonstrating compliance with 75/324/EEC before market placement?</h4>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 0;">The manufacturer or, for imported products, the entity placing the aerosol dispenser on the EU market bears responsibility for demonstrating compliance. This includes maintaining technical documentation covering pressure test results, formulation flammability data, and quality control records, which must be available for inspection by EU market surveillance authorities.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

